Cell["Basic Examples", "Subsection",
 Editable->True,
 TaggingRules->{},
 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",
 CellID->904125756,ExpressionUUID->"8ad9d452-2e30-4456-a46b-e0afaf4735f7"],

Cell["\<\
We start with few well known representations. Matrix representations of \
Clifford algebra can be constructed by two different methods: \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]TensorProduct\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]IdealBasis\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. The \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]TensorProduct\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] product is \
default method, since it is significantly faster. On the other side \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]IdealBasis\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is useful if you \
want to find spinor matrices.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977210259267098*^9, 3.977210286399414*^9}, {
  3.977911663740768*^9, 3.977911882283283*^9}},
 CellID->678410964,ExpressionUUID->"a5a02bed-68a0-413b-b1d2-6a6eb9883893"],

